#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128850 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "915a7c2d-3797-5dfa-8d68-85e2546a23d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11778" :question/problem "Consider a plane lamina of mass $2\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and center of mass $(0,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $2.5\\,\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{m}^2,$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(0,1)$ and the line through the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-0)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =2.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot\\mathrm{m^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128851 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "153b828d-545a-58fb-bc81-0d7311726a87" :question/math-academy-id "e-11779" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda(x, y) =2(x^2+y^2),$ measured in $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ $D$'s center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda(r,\\theta) = 2r^2.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi \\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"}] :question/id #uuid "72ccaeab-ad76-50c7-8b51-7736c5270521" :question/math-academy-id "e-12493" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $4 \\, \\textrm{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $24 \\, \\textrm{kg} \\cdot \\textrm{m}^2,$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = \\pi \\cdot 4^2 = 16\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{2}{16\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{8\\pi}\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 24 & =16+2{d}^{2} \\\\ 2{d}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ d & =2\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "131948d9-d533-5316-bcd3-b7859244892e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12494" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\le x \\le 2, \\: 0 \\le y \\le 4 \\big\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line $l_M$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to $l_M$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and $I_M$ and $I_l$ are the moments of inertia about the lines $l_M$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{3}8\\,\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}^2,\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right) = \\left( \\dfrac{0+2}2, \\dfrac{0+4}2 \\right) = (1,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{8}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-2)^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12(x-1)^{2}+16\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[4(x-1)^{3}+16x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(4+32-(-4+0)) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+3 \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0878c284-4879-55be-912a-9656fbbc7c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431715} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdc4a8e-a57f-57e0-bb50-35bd39671509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431724} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45685d-72c3-5e27-a219-4717f3dcc1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "82.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431732} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6a1caf-3346-52e6-8e61-695021764e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431733} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f59f74-fc37-54c2-9b7b-14e1f15441b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdc4a8e-a57f-57e0-bb50-35bd39671509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431724} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca129e4f-bfea-5bce-a18f-8b24e0ad149a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2270513f-c6f6-52b5-a614-102c61f4f73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163169" :question/problem "Consider a plane lamina of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ and center of mass $(1,1),$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through the point $(-3,4)?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the line through the center of mass $(1,1)$ and the line through the point $(-3,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-3-1)^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.5+{5}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.5+75 \\\\  & =76.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $76.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0993c715-5235-5e81-99f8-e0df49df41fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431716} {:answer/id #uuid "13c67ee2-4b4a-554c-addc-8d24167bac80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431718} {:answer/id #uuid "217da9bf-7efe-5f53-aedd-b0bd54330075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431719} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2eecdc-200a-5731-8433-4d7de3a0d3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431735} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec20448-d6e9-5faa-8568-2a10c85abae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e2eecdc-200a-5731-8433-4d7de3a0d3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431735} :answer-field/id #uuid "329972dd-0743-503f-b3b1-1ba800c2b1fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05ff7fe5-2288-5939-b55a-de668286bd7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163220" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3\\,\\text{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $39\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{6}{9\\pi }=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{81}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 39 & =27+6{d}^{2} \\\\ 6{d}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =2 \\\\ d & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "764d4bb7-3ff1-5f4b-acc9-78c80102bfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431727} {:answer/id #uuid "82451b16-4f7c-56b9-8377-2102d860c661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431728} {:answer/id #uuid "a0529374-1496-5e12-843a-0505f36865b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431740} {:answer/id #uuid "d6536ccb-e3d4-55c3-b971-e406d90e2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431747} {:answer/id #uuid "d996a8d9-ac23-5775-b502-1c0e09b1c5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82451b16-4f7c-56b9-8377-2102d860c661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431728} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb70cd3-bd0e-53c1-a34c-a4e5cb97073f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f698f1e-e965-5c52-9d4f-e9787cc01568" :question/math-academy-id "q-163814" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =2+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2+16 \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a863b7c-edfd-5ed3-b733-c20e15e56c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3e}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431717} {:answer/id #uuid "56e8f7cf-5f92-5e36-9bcd-9ee37ee18eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431725} {:answer/id #uuid "874f21f4-6ea1-564f-aaae-2fe439042176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (3-\\frac{5}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431731} {:answer/id #uuid "913539ca-2d7a-54d5-b8ee-0ae5cbab6a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2-\\frac{3}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431737} {:answer/id #uuid "e13ceabb-d833-566f-a6f9-760558e61eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6e}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "913539ca-2d7a-54d5-b8ee-0ae5cbab6a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2-\\frac{3}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431737} :answer-field/id #uuid "759b288f-9c42-51b4-b2fb-e9f142c0ea9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b0cf2d4-19bf-5bdf-8414-97d0c52886bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163817" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)={e}^{-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )={e}^{-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{e})\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{1}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{e}^{-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({r}^{2}+1){e}^{-{r}^{2}}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{e})\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e})+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\pi (1-\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\pi (1-\\frac{2}{e}+1-\\frac{1}{e}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{3}{e})\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3179da0e-3638-5470-946e-1ba68d8e7a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.1\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431721} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2581c2-d3fa-5830-911b-7de2d4f92b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431734} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5659c0-08e0-5718-a418-64e181a7e44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431749} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb61c04-31bf-503e-9b2e-5e242fc9850f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431750} {:answer/id #uuid "f059b9c9-6fc6-5a69-9ab0-e34a4c12e8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.7\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f059b9c9-6fc6-5a69-9ab0-e34a4c12e8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.7\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431754} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bca215-1091-578b-aa86-b658265555c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cecef3e-71f6-5736-b423-67aa582494ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-163823" :question/problem "Consider a plane lamina of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}.$ If the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass is $1.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ what is the moment of inertia of the lamina about an axis parallel to the first passing through a point on the lamina at a distance of $20\\,\\text{cm}$ from its center of mass?" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=20\\,\\text{cm}=0.2\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.5+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1.5+0.2 \\\\  & =1.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $1.7\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59e32abc-46af-5bfb-8a64-d906d04f1614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431726} {:answer/id #uuid "82747faa-ed1e-5a88-b125-47a8d260239f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431729} {:answer/id #uuid "95a1c2ed-0b14-59fe-afd7-b201b865e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431738} {:answer/id #uuid "a9aebf2a-4542-5040-a04e-0799607a819d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431742} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ac6750-2ae1-5841-aad5-508161e918ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82747faa-ed1e-5a88-b125-47a8d260239f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431729} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef625286-2a13-50df-81d4-0c23373fdb51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a98b9898-9990-5bdc-a565-d49f3ea3b73d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164143" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=4\\cdot 4=16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{16}=\\frac{9}{32}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+4}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(2,2)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}9(x-2)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[9(x-2)^{2}y+3(y-2)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{4}36(x-2)^{2}+48\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{[12(x-2)^{3}+48x]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(96+192-(-96)) \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =12+{2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12+18 \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e8cfaf-fd1b-55f7-8116-1f0008be7b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431723} {:answer/id #uuid "b78b6c21-f632-5a26-b72d-a537bfece296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431745} {:answer/id #uuid "c751f541-41e0-5201-93fb-107f49f6b75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431746} {:answer/id #uuid "e025c665-16ab-588f-8312-dd91f1944aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431751} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9e8082-d12a-577b-aaf0-a262ae52759f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e025c665-16ab-588f-8312-dd91f1944aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431751} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d9aa89-1ce2-5fa3-ba0a-15ff00f5c864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d33e1be-cff3-50e5-94dd-6992342f9938" :question/math-academy-id "q-164146" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{12}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. The moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $\\frac{31}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nGiven that the center of mass of the plate is at $(1,4),$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{12}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{6}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 2\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}((x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}+16y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+8{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}18(x-1)^{2}+324-576+288\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2}18{x}^{2}-36x+54\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[6{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+54x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(48-72+108) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 84 \\\\  & =7\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{31}{4} & =7+3{d}^{2} \\\\ 3{d}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7be1d0-d901-57d1-9262-308cd4b16cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431720} {:answer/id #uuid "33730752-03eb-5584-b1de-cb94ac16c08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431722} {:answer/id #uuid "85a7edbf-02fe-5b24-a716-394fd89efa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431730} {:answer/id #uuid "9a696841-434f-5e2a-ac85-3b84f6b2c204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431739} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ce1d9b-5c76-570c-a685-19b16bac65bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f7be1d0-d901-57d1-9262-308cd4b16cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431720} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d3c1b5c-5f38-591b-b4aa-e017dca1a997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "883c2bc8-ea50-58fe-851b-c16e2097cdb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-164148" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 6\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{6}x,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nGiven that the center of mass is at $(2,3),$ find the moment of inertia about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}((x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2})\\frac{x}{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{6}x(x-2)^{2}+x(y-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[x(x-2)^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}x(y-3)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6x(x-2)^{2}+18x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+42x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+21{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{243}{2}-216+189-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{189}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{4}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
